\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(a) Proposition 2.E.1 (Mas-Colell): 
\series default
If the Walrasian deman function 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is homogenuous of degree zero, then for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\align center
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{L}\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial p_{k}}p_{k}+\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial w}w=0\ \mbox{for}\ l=1,\ldots,L}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In matrix notation, this is expressed as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\align center
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}x(p,w)p+D_{w}x(p,w)w=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\emph on
Proof:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The demand function associated with a single-valued Walrasian choice structure
 is homogeneous of degree zero, 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \alpha>0,\ x(\alpha p,\alpha w)=x(p,w)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\iff\forall\ \alpha>0,\ x(\alpha p,\alpha w)-x(p,w)=0$
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(1)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note, 
\begin_inset Formula $x(\alpha p,\alpha w)=\left[\begin{array}{c}
x_{1}(\alpha p_{1},\ldots,\alpha p_{L},\alpha w)\\
\vdots\\
x_{L}(\alpha p_{1},\ldots,\alpha p_{L},\alpha w)
\end{array}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Define a function 
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathbb{R}_{+}\longrightarrow\mbox{\mathbb{R}}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\alpha)=(\alpha p,\alpha w)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, the demand function can be written as 
\begin_inset Formula $x(\alpha p,\alpha w)=x(f(\alpha))$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Differentiating 
\begin_inset Formula $(1)$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 yields:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\align center
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{L}\frac{\partial x_{l}(\alpha p,\alpha w)}{\partial p_{k}}p_{k}+\frac{\partial x_{l}(\alpha p,\alpha w)}{\partial w}w=0\ \mbox{for}\ l=1,\ldots,L}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note, we used the chain rule to obtain the desired result:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathbb{R}_{+}\longrightarrow\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $x_{l}:\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}\longrightarrow\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\left(x_{l}\circ f\right):\mathbb{R}_{+}\longrightarrow\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\mbox{Note,\ }p_{1}=\alpha p_{1},\ldots,\ p_{L}=\alpha p_{L},\ w=\alpha w\right)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial x_{l}(f(\alpha))}{\partial\alpha}=\frac{\partial x_{1}}{\partial p_{1}}\frac{\partial p_{1}}{\partial\alpha}+\ldots+\frac{\partial x_{L}}{\partial p_{L}}\frac{\partial p_{L}}{\partial\alpha}+\frac{\partial x_{l}}{\partial w}\frac{\partial w}{\partial\alpha}\ \mbox{for}\ l=0,\ldots,L$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In matrix notation, the chain rule is applied as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $D_{\alpha}(x\circ f)=Dx(f(\alpha))Df(\alpha)=Dx(\alpha p,\alpha w)\left[\begin{array}{c}
p\\
w
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
D_{p}x(\alpha p,\alpha w) & D_{w}x(\alpha p,\alpha w)\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
p\\
w
\end{array}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\mbox{The Chain Rule:}\ \frac{\partial x(\alpha p,\alpha w)}{\partial\alpha}=\frac{\partial x(\alpha p,\alpha w)}{\partial p}\frac{\partial p}{\partial\alpha}+\frac{\partial x(\alpha p,\alpha w)}{\partial w}\frac{\partial w}{\partial\alpha}\right)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The final result, in matrix notation, is expressed as:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\align center
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}x(\alpha p,\alpha w)p+D_{w}x(\alpha p,\alpha w)w=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Evaluate at 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=1$
\end_inset

 and we obtain our desired result.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Q.E.D.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(b) Proposition 2.E.2 (Mas-Colell):
\series default
 If the Walrasian deman function satisfies Walras' law, then for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\align center
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \sum_{l=1}^{L}p_{l}\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial p_{k}}+x_{k}(p,w)=0\ \mbox{for}\ k=1,\ldots,L}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
or, written in matrix notation,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\align center
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot D_{p}x(p,w)+x(p,w)^{T}=0^{T}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\emph on
Proof:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' law, 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p,w)=w$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}x_{1}(p,w)+\ldots+p_{L}x_{L}(p,w)=w$
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(2)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Differentiating 
\begin_inset Formula $(2)$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}$
\end_inset

 yields:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial(p\cdot x(p,w))}{\partial p_{1}}=p_{1}\frac{\partial x_{1}(p,w)}{\partial p_{1}}+x_{1}(p,w)+p_{2}\frac{\partial x_{2}(p,w)}{\partial p_{1}}+\dots+p_{L}\frac{\partial x_{L}(p,w)}{\partial p_{1}}=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\implies$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \sum_{l=1}^{L}p_{l}\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial p_{k}}+x_{k}(p,w)=0\ \mbox{for}\ k=1,\ldots,L}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can also obtian the desired result using matrix notation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Define 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p):\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\longrightarrow\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p)=p$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Also, 
\begin_inset Formula $g(p):\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\longrightarrow\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $g(p)=x(p,\bar{w})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, we can define 
\begin_inset Formula $f(p):\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\longrightarrow\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $f(p)=h(p)\cdot g(p)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From (M.A.3) in the mathematical appendix of Mas-Colell,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\align center
\begin_inset Formula $Df(p)=h(p)\cdot Dg(p)+g(p)\cdot Dh(p)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From the above product rule, we obtain:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\align center
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}\left[p\cdot x(p,w)\right]=p\cdot D_{p}x(p,w)+x(p,w)^{T}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, the final result, in matrix notation, is expressed as:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\align center
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot D_{p}x(p,w)+x(p,w)^{T}=0^{T}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Q.E.D.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
